Q: WPF control ignores properties when used in a template A: <p>I'm writing this based on my own experience.
Perhaps it's somewhere in the documentation, but I couldn't find it right away, and I didn't bother searching deeply.</p>
<p>Let's take this example:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Window ----------------&gt;
    &lt;UniformGrid Columns=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
        &lt;ContentControl&gt;
            &lt;local:MySlider x:Name=&quot;slider&quot; Minimum=&quot;-100&quot; Maximum=&quot;100&quot;/&gt;
        &lt;/ContentControl&gt;
        &lt;ContentControl&gt;
            &lt;ContentControl.ContentTemplate&gt;
                &lt;DataTemplate&gt;
                    &lt;local:MySlider x:Name=&quot;slider&quot; Minimum=&quot;-100&quot; Maximum=&quot;100&quot;/&gt;
                &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
            &lt;/ContentControl.ContentTemplate&gt;
        &lt;/ContentControl&gt;
    &lt;/UniformGrid&gt;
&lt;/Window&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Everything seems identical – the only difference is whether it's used in content or in a template. But this similarity is only apparent.</p>
<p>When defining an element in content, the element is created and initialized similarly to how it's done in C#: the element is created using the default constructor, and all the settings in that constructor are applied accordingly; then, the properties individually specified in the XAML tag are initialized. Since the properties are initialized AFTER the constructor is called, the initialized values ​​will be final.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    MySlider slider = new();
    slider.SetValue(MySlider.MinimumProperty, -100);
    slider.SetValue(MySlider.MaximumProperty, 100);
</code></pre>
<p>If a Template is created (whether it's a data template or a control template), then no elements are created directly. A Template is a FACTORY of elements.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    FrameworkElementFactory sliderFactory = new FrameworkElementFactory(typeof(MySlider));
    sliderFactory.SetValue(FrameworkElement.NameProperty, &quot;slider&quot;);
    sliderFactory.SetValue(MySlider.MinimumProperty, -100.0);
    sliderFactory.SetValue(MySlider.MaximumProperty, 100.0);

    DataTemplate dataTemplate = new DataTemplate();
    dataTemplate.VisualTree = sliderFactory;
</code></pre>
<p>Later, when the template is used, a factory <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.frameworktemplate.loadcontent?view=netframework-4.8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LoadContent()</a> method is called that returns the root element of the visual tree, and it is during this method call that the constructors are invoked.</p>
<p>Property values ​​specified in the template have lower priority than local values. See points 3 and 4. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/desktop/wpf/properties/dependency-property-value-precedence#dependency-property-precedence-list" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Dependency property precedence list</a></p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong> I advise you to change the default values ​​instead of setting local values:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    public class MySlider : Slider
    {
        static MySlider()
        {
            MinimumProperty.OverrideMetadata(
                typeof(MySlider),
                new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(0.0));

            MaximumProperty.OverrideMetadata(
                typeof(MySlider),
                new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(255.0));
        }
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Surprising behaviour of using static_cast on struct with bit fields A: <blockquote>
<p>So it was a &quot;recent&quot; addition to the standard then?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, C++20 allows aggregate initalization with <code>(..)</code> instead of <code>{..}</code>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is there a way then to initialize such a struct without resorting to reinterpret_cast or std::bit_cast?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Add proper constructor to the struct</p>
<pre><code>struct byte
{
    explicit byte(unsigned char c) : high(c &gt;&gt; 4), low(c &amp; 0x0F) {}

    unsigned char high : 4;
    unsigned char low : 4;
};
</code></pre>
